# Cursor Rules — iracing-obs-switcher (Python 3.11+)

## Project context
This is a long-running Python service + external TUI.
It integrates:
- iRacing via pyirsdk (shared memory, polling)
- OBS via obs-websocket v5 (network IO)
- async HTTP + WebSocket API (aiohttp)
- Textual-based TUI (separate process)

Stability > elegance.
Determinism > cleverness.

## Python baseline
- Python >= 3.11
- Async-first mindset (aiohttp, async state machine)
- No blocking calls in async loops
- Explicit typing for all public functions and state objects

## Architectural rules
- iracing/ = pure data extraction, NO business logic
- obs/ = thin client wrapper, no policy
- logic/ = state machine + decision rules ONLY
- server/ = API glue, never drives logic directly
- tui/ = read-only client, no authority

Never mix responsibilities across these layers.

## State machine rules
- State transitions must be explicit and testable.
- No hidden side effects inside getters.
- All scene switches go through ONE decision path.
- Cooldowns / debounce must be time-based, not frame-based.

## Error handling
- iRacing disconnected is a NORMAL state, not an error.
- OBS disconnect should retry with backoff.
- Never crash the main loop on external failure.
- Log reasoned decisions (why a scene switched).

## Async rules
- No asyncio.create_task without ownership.
- Background tasks must be cancellable.
- Use monotonic time for cooldown logic.
- Never sleep blindly; always explain why a sleep exists.

## Logging
- Use structured, intent-based logs:
  - state_changed
  - scene_switch
  - override_applied
  - connection_lost / connection_restored
- Avoid noisy per-frame logs.

## Tests
- pytest only
- Freezegun for time-based logic
- Mock pyirsdk and obs client, never real connections
- Test state machine independently of IO

## Dependencies
- Do NOT introduce new dependencies unless explicitly requested.
- Prefer stdlib over helper libs.

## Style
- Be concise.
- Prefer readable over clever.
- No speculative refactors.
- Minimal diff unless refactor is explicitly requested.

## Deliverables
When implementing:
- code
- tests (or explicit reason why not)
- short explanation of behavior

When refactoring:
- preserve observable behavior
- explain invariants
